
linux-assoc-array-fixes-v2
https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tag/?h=assoc-array-fixes-v2

##

Clean up assoc_array library

Nils Ollrogge reported a bug in the assoc_array library and had a patch
to fix it too, but that code was too obscure for me to follow, so I did
what I preach: use modern LLM tools to help make sense of it all.

So this is an LLM-assisted branch which makes the code more readable by
splitting up some of the larger and hard-to-read functions.  The code
was then run through a test harness in user space, which not only
confirmed the bug Nils reported, but found three other bugs too - all
going back to the very first commit adding this library back in 2013.

v2: David Howells asked for a fix-up of a couple of comments and rename
assoc_array_segment_leaves() to assoc_array_fill_segment_cache(), and
added his Tested-by: and Reviewed-by:

##

assoc_array: correct the documentation of get_key_chunk()'s level argument

assoc_array.rst says the level passed to get_key_chunk() will be a
multiple of ASSOC_ARRAY_KEY_CHUNK_SIZE, and asks for the chunk starting
at that bit.  assoc_array_walk() after a shortcut, and both insertion
paths, pass the level of a node, which is any multiple of
ASSOC_ARRAY_LEVEL_STEP.  Each then shifts the chunk it gets back right
by level & ASSOC_ARRAY_KEY_CHUNK_MASK, so what it wants is the chunk
that contains that bit.

The keyring code copes, because keyring_get_key_chunk() divides the
level by ASSOC_ARRAY_KEY_CHUNK_SIZE before using it.  An implementation
written to the documentation does not: one that returns a one-word key
for level 0 and nothing for any other level files leaves under the
wrong slot the first time a node is split, and a later insertion hits
the BUG_ON() after found_slot_for_multiple_occupancy in
assoc_array_insert_into_terminal_node().

Say what the code expects.  get_object_key_chunk() is documented as
"the previous function", so this covers it too.

